SM(486,{"n":"Acts 27","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 27.mp3","d":2275.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.52,"In just a minute, we're going to be picking up in uh Acts chapter 27."],[28.0,"It's been a little bit."],[29.76,"As always, the first Bible study on the book of Acts is available online if you want to go back and listen to that for flashback purposes."],[41.12,"We're getting here toward the end of the book."],[43.2,"So but you can go and listen to my introduction there."],[47.12,"To get us caught up to where we are now, we've been going through a whole series of St."],[52.32,"Paul's trials. Sort of one after the other after his arrest in Jerusalem."],[59.4,"He returned to Jerusalem for the Feast of Pentecost."],[63.4,"And while he was there, some people showed up to make trouble, who had been making trouble for him over the course of his missionary journeys."],[73.24,"And he was going at St."],[76.04,"James's suggestion to go and sponsor a group of men who were taking Nazirite vows at the temple to kind of do a public show of the fact that St."],[85.56,"Paul was not rejecting or teaching against Jewish traditions, but that he was embracing them."],[93.24,"Despite that attempt, when he got into the temple, people started spreading rumors in the streets that he had brought a Greek into the temple, Gentile, and defiled it and spreading various rumors about him."],[104.84,"Paul And this led to a mob that decided to beat St."],[112.0,"He was sort of rescued by some Roman soldiers who were also intending to beat him to find out what was going on until they found out that St."],[120.48,"Paul was a Roman citizen."],[122.4,"Once they found that out, they sort of put him under house arrest in the barracks."],[139.52,"And so the uh Centuries, the commander of the guard, sent him on to the capital to uh Caesarea to the governor, uh, so that the governor could deal with it."],[149.28,"Paul would be out of the immediate danger"],[150.04,"And so that uh St."],[153.8,"Um governor was replaced by another governor uh who continued to keep uh St."],[160.76,"Paul in prison."],[162.12,"Uh he had a number of trials, he was sort of trotted out when, as we saw, when Herod Agrippa came to visit the governor, uh, because the uh the Roman governor, as we talked about, wasn't really clear on exactly what was going on."],[177.64,"He knew it had something to do with the Jewish religion, but he didn't know much about the Jewish religion."],[182.12,"U,h and so the reason he trotted St."],[184.04,"Paul out was he was hoping that Herod Agrippa maybe could explain to him what this was all about."],[190.04,"Uh St. Paul had already appealed to Caesar, which was his right as a Roman citizen."],[194.44,"We talked about how that didn't necessarily mean you'd ever actually get to see Caesar."],[194.84,"Uh, but but that's St"],[200.48,"Paul's goal, as we've seen for some time, even before his arrest, he had said that his goal was to go to Rome and his hope was to be able to proclaim the gospel to Caesar."],[209.12,"And he's been using all of these trials as opportunities to proclaim the gospel to the people who are putting him on trial, including Herod Agrippa, including two Roman governors, etc."],[219.06,"But uh because of that, the governor had to uh explain to send a letter explaining to Caesar why this person was on trial and why he was sending him to Rome, and he couldn't really come up with anything because um whatever was going on, he uh seemed to. to think had more do with Jewish laws and customs than Roman law, which St"],[244.94,"Paul hadn't obviously violated in any way."],[249.1,"And so uh Herod Agrippa uh wasn't really able to help him."],[254.06,"Uh he say he he commented that St."],[257.42,"Paul had very nearly converted him."],[259.74,"He said you think to convert me in such a short time, you know, to Christianity."],[263.66,"Uh, but basically told uh the governor I I got nothing."],[268.7,"Uh in terms of why, what charge."],[271.74,"He says uh it should have been set free, except for appeal to seaesar."],[274.94,"Yeah, it was having appealed to Caesar."],[276.06,"Yeah. So he said, I I got nothing in terms of Roman law."],[279.82,"So I don't know."],[282.7,"But uh the governor is still responsible one way or another."],[286.1,"Uh-huh. If someone appeals to Caesar and he didn't send him on to Rome, then again, he would be in trouble for violating protocol."],[293.64,"And so as we begin in chapter 27, they're going to be sort of packing St."],[297.4,"Paul off to send him to send him to Rome."],[302.68,"And as we saw during this whole trial process, which has gone on for quite some time."],[307.4,"We see this has gone on for years."],[308.84,"I mean, it's been a few chapters and just seems like one trial after another."],[312.28,"But this has been a couple of this has been a few years that St."],[314.84,"Paul has been in prison."],[315.8,"In other words, like legal procedures today."],[319.08,"And uh he's been under primarily a form of house arrest in the sense that he's been kept within the Roman territory, but it's been mainly sort of protective custody, because they're aware that there are still people out there trying to trying to kill him for reasons they don't entirely understand."],[337.84,"And so they're keeping him there protected, but he's receiving visitors, he's able to write."],[341.92,"Some of his epistles are written during this period."],[344.48,"Um he's been able to communicate freely, he just hasn't been able to come and go."],[349.28,"Um more for his own safety at this point than for anything else."],[352.64,"Because again, if it got back to Rome that somebody had appealed to Caesar and you'd let them be assassinated in prison, a Roman citizen, that would not go well for you as a Roman governor."],[363.52,"So they have to protect him as well."],[366.8,"So as we pick up here, uh St."],[368.88,"Paul is about to begin his uh journey uh by boat to Rome, unless there are any other questions or comments or recriminations."],[373.52,"Okay Uh Acts of the Apostles, chapter 27, verse 1."],[384.92,"And when it was decided that we should sail to Italy, they delivered Paul and some other prisoners to one named Julius, a centurion of the Augustine regiment."],[396.36,"So the Augustan regiment is pretty much the most important uh regiment that has any representatives in the area."],[407.88,"The Praetorian Guard who guards Caesar himself is the most important element of the Roman military."],[413.4,"But in terms of in an outlying province like Judea, these are the VIP officers in the in the Roman military."],[423.44,"And so they hand him over."],[424.96,"He's put in the custody of uh Julius."],[428.08,"So now it's Julius' responsibility to keep him safe and get him to Rome."],[435.68,"So entering a ship of Adramitrium, we put to sea, meaning to sail along the coasts of Asia."],[447.68,"Along the coast of Asia."],[448.96,"Okay, so as we mentioned before, they don't sail straight across the Mediterranean at this time in history."],[455.6,"So when they say along the coast of Asia, what they mean by Asia is Asia Minor, which is what's now Turkey."],[461.36,"So they're planning to sail up the coast and then of Palestine and then uh over across and around, and then they'll go around Greece and then go over to Italy, over toward Rome."],[478.52,"Aristarchus, a Macedonian of Thessalonica, was with us."],[482.52,"So the us, remember the us means Saint Luke is there again now, too."],[488.04,"So St. Luke is."],[489.32,"Why would St."],[489.96,"Luke even be allowed to accompany Paul?"],[493.96,"Well, for the same reason he was allowed to receive visitors."],[496.92,"Oh, okay. So he can actually."],[498.92,"I mean, the Romans are gonna pay to take Oh, they're uh they're not paying for them."],[504.2,"This is this is not a Roman military ship."],[507.0,"Okay. That's what that's why they mentioned it was a a ship of Adrametrium."],[511.26,"So the Centurion bought passage."],[514.06,"Okay. He would have to buy passage for St."],[515.9,"Paul. Everybody else is paying for their own who wants to come along."],[520.06,"Um but yeah, this is a merchant ship."],[522.3,"In fact, the centurion may not have had to actually pay, he may have just sort of commented and said, You're taking us to Rome."],[528.54,"Being in the military."],[529.82,"Um but yeah, St."],[530.94,"Luke and anybody else coming along and this Aristarchus of Thessalodaga, they would have paid their own, had to pay their own way."],[538.14,"But because it's just a merchant ship, they could do that."],[542.46,"Uh have we heard of the Saristarchus before?"],[547.58,"I mean, uh no."],[549.82,"Okay, so but he is a Christian, obviously."],[552.4,"That's why we've got this why he's identified as a Macedonian of Thessalonica."],[558.12,"So he's ethnically Macedonian, lives in lives in Thessalonica."],[562.92,"So presumably he's one of the people who was converted by St."],[566.84,"Paul's uh mission there."],[571.24,"And the next day we landed at Sidon, so this is up the coast, uh part of what's now Lebanon."],[578.44,"Uh and Julius treated Paul kindly and gave him liberty to go to his friends and receive care."],[586.04,"So Julius is one of the good guys in terms of treating him decently."],[592.04,"So when they put him to port, he's letting St."],[594.28,"Paul visit with people."],[595.96,"Because of course, St."],[596.92,"Paul knows people in most of these churches from his previous journeys."],[601.12,"And so he's allowed to visit with people there and uh have again a little bit of freedom."],[609.04,"When we were put to sea from there, we sailed under the shelter of Cyprus because the winds were contrary."],[617.36,"So the winds are going in the wrong direction."],[621.12,"To take them along the route they wanted to, and so they kind of loop around around Cyprus, deviating from their original intended course."],[633.72,"We had sailed over the sea which is off Cilicia and Pamphylia, we came to Myra, a city of Lycia."],[640.6,"Okay. Or Lycia."],[642.56,"Nicholas, since we're coming up on St"],[644.36,"That is the city where St."],[647.8,"Nicholas Day, that is the Myra in Lycia where St."],[651.08,"Nicholas was later bishop."],[652.52,"Uh-huh. Oh, Nicholas of Myra."],[654.76,"Yeah, so it's a it's a good uh 250 odd years later."],[660.84,"That St. Nicholas is the bishop there, but then it is the same uh the same city."],[666.44,"There, the centurion found an Alexandrian ship sailing to Italy, and he put us on board."],[671.9,"So there he says, okay, I found another guy."],[674.14,"And this is this is nor uh uh the normal way passengers got places at this time in hit history."],[680.86,"There weren't sort of big passenger liners that would take people."],[684.94,"But there were all kinds of merchant ships going all over them carrying things from place to place."],[687.34,"going. Is now back"],[693.96,"So now they need another one too."],[695.45,"And so you would just book passage."],[697.69,"You would just pay them something to take you along."],[700.09,"You know. Like bargain."],[701.69,"Yeah. Either either you'd pay them money or you'd volunteer to work the ship."],[706.57,"Work the ship, you know, or do something else they needed."],[709.37,"Barter, you know, um, in order to get passage."],[716.01,"When we had sailed slowly many days and arrived with difficulty off Catus."],[723.57,"The wind not permitting us to proceed, we sailed under the shelter of Crete off Salmone."],[729.37,"Okay, so again, they're still having problems with the winds not going their way."],[731.45,"s. And so that makes everything they can keep making progress, but it' slow"],[737.97,"Usually that's during winter."],[739.75,"Yeah. So they're making this very slow progress, and they keep sort of detouring around, like they're gonna detour around Crete the same way they detoured around Cyprus because they're sort of using the uh the island as a windbreak."],[754.71,"Uh that's what it means by under the shelter of."],[757.19,"Because it's better to have no wind than to have winds blowing in the opposite direction."],[761.99,"Right if you're trying to make trying to make progress."],[764.87,"You can at least use oars"],[766.55,"Yeah. Passing it with difficulty, we came to a place called Fair Havens near the city of Lesia."],[774.79,"So they had real estate developers even then."],[775.83,"Tourist spot Yes, Fair Havens."],[782.43,"Yeah. No parking to it."],[786.59,"That has a long history."],[787.95,"It's an old folks' home."],[788.91,"I mean, that's how Greenland became Greenland."],[790.83,"Yeah. It's just not so not so green."],[796.17,"Now, when much time had been spent and sailing was now dangerous because the fast was already over."],[802.89,"Paul advised them saying, Men, I perceive that this voyage will end with disaster and much loss."],[808.97,"What fast is almost over?"],[811.37,"This would be, it's referring to the uh winter fest, which would have been before the uh the uh before what we now call Hanukkah."],[823.09,"So this is in the winter period."],[824.77,"So this is the same thing."],[825.25,"And so he's saying the weather's getting weather's getting worse."],[828.21,"Uh-huh. Right is the idea."],[829.63,"So it's around it's around this time of year."],[831.39,"Yeah. So the weather's weather's getting weather's getting worse, not better, and they're still having trouble making progress."],[837.93,"And so St."],[838.65,"Paul says, uh, folks, you know, not only are we not making a lot of progress, but if we actually keep trying to make it, this could go really badly for us all."],[849.77,"Um we we don't have the same uh people who are terrified of flying have the same feeling when they're on airplanes, but we don't have the same sort of fear of the sea that they did at this time in history."],[866.77,"And if we had their boat, then we would understand why."],[870.38,"Um there's a reason why they didn't try and sail straight across the Mediterranean because they wouldn't have made it."],[877.66,"So yeah, the the sea, this is why, you know, in the Old Testament, when we loop back around and get into the Old Testament, not just in the Old Testament, but in all the ancient Near East, all the nations around it, the sea is this symbol of like chaos and death and destruction because it's you know, storms come in off the sea and destroy things, and all of a sudden without people go out to sea and never come back, and there's creatures living in the sea that we don't know about anything about, you know."],[907.34,"And yeah, and it's dark and mysterious, and humans can't really go there, and so it's very frightening and scary."],[914.74,"In fact, Yom, which is the Hebrew word for sea, is also the name of the uh basically the most evil Canaanite god who's like you know so and and there's a little bit of that, even I mean there they're more than a little of that in the New Testament and some of the symbolism and things."],[931.54,"Um we'll talk about that as we go."],[934.18,"There are already some examples in the gospel, like with Jesus walking on the water."],[937.78,"I've run across it in the Renaissance."],[939.7,"Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah."],[941.38,"Uh I mean, Pope Pius was scared to death at the sea and would rather do anything than get on a boat."],[949.3,"We'll just have had uh a different way of like say for the the the Greek god Poseidon would would would I imagine they would they would look at Poseidon a bit more menacingly than other gods because of this."],[963.74,"Yes. Rather than it's oh he's God of the sea, fish Aquaman."],[967.58,"No, it wasn't Aquaman."],[971.1,"No. It was that's why he was one of the three most Poseidon was one of the three most powerful gods of Greek mythology."],[977.8,"Yeah. And he was it was a force to be reckoned with that."],[980.92,"Yeah. So the the the sea is sort of the spreading thing."],[983.64,"The place one place where that shows up in the New Testament, though, is is the word abyss."],[989.16,"The word abyss means the depths of the sea."],[991.8,"Oh. Uh-huh. But that's sort of where hell was."],[997.61,"Because remember the the the demons when Christ casts them out and and they go into the pigs, they say, Are you come to cast us into the abyss before then they run up run end up running in off a crag into the sea anyway."],[1010.06,"That's the idea that they went back where they came from."],[1013.1,"The book of Revelation will see the angel takes the key to the abyss and opens it, and all the demons come flying out of it."],[1019.9,"I'd always thought of abyss as just sort of a big chasm, but it's it's right."],[1023.98,"No, it's the depths of the sea."],[1025.66,"Yeah. Wow, okay."],[1027.82,"Uh and so yeah, they down there was bad stuff."],[1034.36,"And the way they the way they thought, we'll talk about this more when we loop around and get into Genesis, but the way in the ancient Near East, including the ancient Hebrews, thought the world looked, was that it was basically flat."],[1047.82,"And it was surrounded by water."],[1050.3,"There was sort of a dome of the sky over it."],[1052.54,"But the water also went underneath."],[1055.26,"And there were these pillars down there in the water that supported like the dry land."],[1061.58,"Okay, those were referred to as the pillars of the earth."],[1064.64,"And so down in there, there were that water down there, that's the underworld."],[1070.27,"That's the abyss."],[1071.23,"That's the when Jonah gets swallowed by the fish, it says that the fish swam down to the pillars of the earth."],[1077.55,"The idea is he went down into the underworld."],[1080.51,"He does this descent into the descent into the underworld."],[1084.05,"Which is so there's there's a little more to uh when Christ talks about the sign of Jonah and compares his death and resurrection in three days."],[1093.45,"It's not just that he was in a fish for three, not just the three days thing."],[1097.55,"There's more to it than that."],[1098.99,"That there was this descent into the underworld and death involved from which then uh Jonah was freed."],[1108.51,"But yeah, so the the sea is a uh frightening and scary thing."],[1114.43,"There are still tribes that are absolutely freaked out that people swim up with scuba gear and they eat them with sticks and make them go away."],[1121.71,"Yeah. Uh-huh. Yeah."],[1123.55,"Yeah. And so, like like, I said, it's sort of like the way they looked at the sea, is sort of like the way people who are terrified of flying look at getting on a plane."],[1129.47,"That's and that's so that's that's under good conditions"],[1130.63,"You know, and sort of white knuckling it, you know."],[1136.63,"There are a lot of people who don't like sailing."],[1138.95,"So these are really bad conditions."],[1140.55,"And so St."],[1141.19,"Paul is saying this is all gonna end badly if we keep trying to make progress with this kind of weather at this time of year."],[1155.59,"Okay, and much loss not only of the cargo and ship, but also our lives."],[1165.83,"So the point, the point there is what he's saying to the sailors is right, I'm not just talking about like you might have to throw the cargo overboard or your ship might get damaged, which is your livelihood."],[1176.83,"We might all die."],[1178.03,"Uh-huh. So, you know, losing losing your cargo is bad, losing your ship is worse."],[1182.65,"Losing your life is way worse."],[1185.45,"Yeah. Or drowning."],[1187.29,"You know, yeah."],[1188.41,"This is gonna be worse."],[1189.45,"Or freezing to death in the water."],[1196.09,"Nevertheless, the centurion was more persuaded by the helmsman and the owner of the ship than by the things spoken by Paul."],[1203.21,"Okay, so the the uh the helmsman says, no, I I got this."],[1208.73,"Uh huh. He's the one steering the ship."],[1210.84,"So he's like, I could we can do this, we can do this."],[1213.0,"And the owner, of course, is like, no, I need to get this cargo to get out of Rome to to get get my my money."],[1219.6,"And so the centurion says, No, keep going."],[1227.12,"And because the harbor was not suitable to winter, to winter in."],[1230.8,"In spite of being called Ferry."],[1232.32,"In spite of being called Fairy, but the majority advised to set sail from there, also."],[1236.66,"So what's St."],[1237.54,"Paul's advising is hey, why don't we just stay here for the winter?"],[1240.42,"Uh huh. Then when the spring comes, we'll set sail again."],[1243.22,"Hopefully, we'll have better winds."],[1244.74,"We'll definitely have better weather."],[1247.38,"Then we could do this then."],[1248.74,"But the the the other people on the boat, it's saying, say, Well, this isn't I I don't want to spend the winter here."],[1254.56,"I don't want to spend three months here."],[1257.52,"So let's try and keep going."],[1260.16,"If by any means they could reach Phoenix, a harbor of Crete opening toward the southwest and northwest and winter there."],[1266.68,"So they say, well, let's at least try and get a little further."],[1270.22,"Because if we have to stop in winter somewhere, this harbor is a better option."],[1275.9,"This is a better place to stop if we end up having to."],[1282.06,"When the south wind blew softly, supposing that they had obtained their desire, pulling out to sea, they sailed close by Crete."],[1290.7,"But not long after, a tempestuous headwind arose called Eurocledon."],[1300.86,"They have a name for the storm."],[1302.46,"Well, it's yeah, it's a well, like we have El Nino."],[1306.8,"It's a seasonal wind."],[1309.37,"Or the Santa Ana winds in California where I grew up."],[1311.93,"My translation says called the Northeastern."],[1314.65,"Yeah. So yeah, that's a more free translation."],[1318.81,"But yeah, that's it's that kind of thing."],[1320.41,"This is a seasonal wind."],[1322.01,"Okay, so they had wind going in the right direction."],[1324.81,"So they think, oh no, this is gonna be okay."],[1326.89,"We'll be able to make it at least to that other port."],[1329.05,"So they set out, and then boom, here comes the nor'easter."],[1337.53,"So when the ship was caught and could not head into the wind, we let her drive and running under the shelter of an island called Clauda, we secured the skiff with difficulty."],[1348.25,"Okay, so they managed to, it's blowing against them and, it's blowing far too hard against them to make any progress"],[1355.33,"So they cut they tack to the side, and they managed to get around the side of another small island called Clauda that they're using as a windbreak, basically, and they managed to kind of get it anchored to shore."],[1370.21,"Or near shore."],[1372.93,"And what's this about the skiff?"],[1376.13,"The skiff would be the boat that they would use to go to go ash let's I can't find it now."],[1385.57,"It says we secured the skiff with difficulty."],[1389.65,"Does this mean that the skiff doesn't kept it from falling off and floating away?"],[1393.97,"Yeah, the the wind is blowing the skiff off the ship."],[1396.29,"Right Right. The ship is gonna get blown apart, basically"],[1400.65,"Because even going to the s right, they've turned to the side, but the wind is now bl hitting the ship from from the side."],[1409.53,"When they had taken it on board, that's the skiff."],[1412.41,"So they pull it off where it's sort of hanging onto the boat."],[1419.85,"They used cables to undergird the ship."],[1422.97,"And reinforce it."],[1424.03,"To reinforce it, run it underneath."],[1426.67,"They're fearing that they should run aground on the Certus Sands, they struck sail and so were driven."],[1434.19,"Okay, so they they're worried that they're gonna run aground, because there's some sand bars nearby."],[1439.95,"They're either gonna get run aground on it. and that even the cables aren't gonna be enough to hold everything together"],[1445.99,"And so they pull down the sails completely."],[1450.65,"And just let let the boat sort of goes where it wants to go as the current carries it."],[1460.49,"And because we were exceedingly tempest tossed, the next day they lightened the ship."],[1465.53,"So they start throwing cargo overboard."],[1469.85,"On the third day, we threw the ship's tackle overboard with our own hands."],[1474.65,"As in the riggings to secure everything on."],[1477.45,"Yeah, all all the anything that isn't nailed down that they don't need to keep the ship holding together, they throw overboard, basically."],[1485.37,"I'd say they th'rowreing with their own hands, that means even like St."],[1486.17,"ing Luke, everybody on the ship is help."],[1488.77,"Paul and St."],[1492.93,"Not just yeah."],[1494.29,"Not just the sailors."],[1496.23,"Not just the centurion, but everybody is pitching it to try and uh try and keep the boat together."],[1505.51,"Now, when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days, and no small tempest beat on us, all hope that we would be saved was finally given up."],[1513.67,"Okay, so they're just now drifting with the current and the winds with the sails down."],[1518.31,"Apparently, this storm, it's so cloudy, they can't see stars, they can't see the sun, so they can't determine where they are."],[1525.25,"They've obviously gotten away from land, so they can't figure out where they are or which direction they need to head or what they need to do."],[1530.61,"So they kind of are like, We're in real trouble now"],[1535.45,"They're sort of adrift."],[1540.89,"But after a long abstinence from food, then Paul stood in the middle of them and said, Men, you should have listened to me and not have sailed from Crete and incurred this disaster and loss."],[1552.57,"So St. Paul is a wonderful saint, but he is that guy who will tell you I told you so."],[1559.85,"I was just thinking how popular that cargo overboard, they probably threw over most of the food too."],[1567.58,"So now they're out there floating with no idea which way to go."],[1570.1,"You don't want to just start sailing in a random direction because you would be sailing out in the middle of the Mediterranean and making it worse."],[1575.22,"So he says, you should have listened to me"],[1580.78,"I I long abstinence from food, I thought, oh sure, he's seasick."],[1586.3,"He's eating. No, this is their out of food."],[1590.46,"That's on top of everything else."],[1592.06,"They were fasting even though they were traveling on the ship."],[1595.34,"No, this is their out of food."],[1596.7,"They run out of food on the ship."],[1598.06,"But before what we read, they were."],[1600.14,"Oh no, that's just talking about the fast."],[1602.38,"Okay. In terms of the time of year."],[1604.46,"But were they allowed to fast if they are traveling?"],[1608.78,"Well, it it doesn't say whether St."],[1610.62,"Paul was fasting or not."],[1612.46,"It was a Jewish fast."],[1615.42,"It would be like if we said after Lent, you know, it's nearly the end of Lent."],[1619.58,"Oh, okay. Just to tell you what time of year it was"],[1623.38,"It wasn't really about whether or not they were actually fasting while they traveled."],[1630.1,"But yeah, now they have to because they don't have any food."],[1636.02,"But after he says, I told you so, St."],[1638.1,"Paul goes on to say, and now I urge you to take heart, for there will be no loss of life among you, but only of the ship."],[1647.62,"He says, I told you so, but don't worry, because the ship is going to end up being lost, but we're all going to get out of this with our lives."],[1657.38,"So if that's a prophecy to them?"],[1659.38,"Well, he's about to explain why he does that."],[1662.42,"For there stood by me this night an angel of the God. to whom I belong and whom I serve, saying, Do not be afraid, Paul"],[1670.46,"You must be brought before Caesar, and indeed God has granted you all those who sail with you."],[1676.06,"So he says, An angel came to me from the God to whom I belong and whom I serve, and said to me, You know, you can tell he's speaking to Gentile pagans, because he has to say, the God."],[1688.14,"The God, which one?"],[1690.06,"You know. But he said, Who told me that I'm gonna make it, I'm gonna make it to Rome, and that that uh all of you are gonna make it there with me eventually."],[1707.42,"Therefore, take heart, men, for I believe God that it will be just as it was told to me."],[1710.46,"However, me what we must run aground on a certain island"],[1717.06,"So he says, Look, what we've got to do is, I've also been told there's an island nearby, we need to just run the boat aground there."],[1725.54,"And then and then wait there."],[1729.38,"Now when the 14th night had come, so this is two weeks now that they've been out there lost at sea, as we were driven up and down in the Adriatic Sea, about midnight, the sailors sensed that they were drawing near some land."],[1746.66,"Okay, so they get the impression that they're approaching land."],[1752.58,"And they took soundings and found it to be 20 fathoms."],[1755.22,"5. And when they had gone a little farther, they took soundings again and found it to be 1 fathoms"],[1761.5,"So this is how you made sure you were approaching land."],[1764.16,"You drop something heavy on a rope and see how deep it is."],[1770.32,"It's getting less deep as they progress."],[1772.16,"So that means they're coming towards land."],[1778.16,"So it goes from 20 to 15, so it's like, oh okay."],[1781.76,"We're coming up towards some land."],[1787.12,"Then fearing lest we should run aground on the rocks, they dropped four anchors from the stern and prayed for day to come."],[1794.96,"So they don't want to keep in the middle of the night just drifting toward land, because you don't know."],[1799.96,"You don't want to, if there's rocks, if there's a sandbar or something away from the land, you don't want to wreck the ship while you're still a ways out."],[1808.36,"So they drop anchors so they won't drift any further."],[1811.48,"They wait for morning."],[1813.08,"And as the sailors were seeking to escape from the ship, when they let down the skiff into the sea under pretense of putting out anchors from the prow."],[1822.2,"So the sailors, they haven't had any food, they've been out there for two weeks."],[1826.36,"They're pretty desperate to get off that boat."],[1828.54,"So they say, Oh, we're gonna put down some more anchors."],[1832.4,"And then they try to steal the skiff and make off, make off toward land, because they just want off the boat right now, and they're like, We'll risk it."],[1842.48,"Paul said to the centurion in the and. the soldiers, unless these men stay ship, you cannot be saved"],[1850.76,"And the soldiers cut away the ropes of the skiff and let it fall down."],[1857.72,"So St. Paul says to them, look, if they take off in the middle of the night in that skiff and don't stay with the boat, I'm not promising you, you know, everybody's gonna make it like I did before."],[1870.26,"So they're listening to him now."],[1872.74,"Well, yeah. He's sort of he's sort of giving them the only hope they have."],[1879.3,"I mean, otherwise the situation is hopeless, and they're all gonna die at sea of starvation or dehydration, or when they run out of fresh water, you know."],[1888.38,"And as day was about to dawn, Paul implored them all to take food, saying, Today is the 14th day, you have waited and continued without food and eaten nothing."],[1899.5,"So he says, Go ahead and eat, meaning they don't have they've been they don't have much food left."],[1904.95,"So they've been only eating minimally to try and make it last as long as possible."],[1909.67,"So he tells them, no, go ahead now and eat."],[1912.41,"Because we're coming to the end of no food."],[1918.33,"Therefore, I urge you to take nourishment, for this is for your survival, since not a hair will fall from the head of any of you."],[1925.61,"You know, obviously he doesn't mean that literally that none of them would ever lose a hair."],[1930.49,"But his point is no harm's going to come to you."],[1933.57,"Go ahead and eat the provisions we have."],[1937.89,"When he had said these things, he took bread and gave thanks to God in the presence of them all, and when he had broken it, he began to eat."],[1947.01,"So even though we don't do this anymore."],[1952.53,"Well, actually, there was St."],[1954.37,"Luke there. So the language that's used here is pretty clear that this is St."],[1959.81,"Paul using some of the bread to celebrate the Eucharist."],[1963.79,"This is the language."],[1964.75,"They took bread and blessed it and broke it."],[1967.01,"That's the same language that's used by St."],[1969.17,"Luke of Christ."],[1970.69,"So there probably only would have been those two communicants with St."],[1975.73,"Paul. Oh, there's the other guy, Aristotle."],[1977.85,"Yeah, so there would have been two along with him."],[1979.93,"Yeah, Aristarchus and St."],[1981.61,"Luke. But there were at least a couple of people."],[1991.37,"And uh just for the record, he doesn't give it to all of the other people on the boat who aren't Christians."],[1998.25,"Just throwing that out."],[2003.45,"Then they were all encouraged and also took food themselves."],[2006.57,"And in all, we were 276 persons on the ship."],[2010.97,"So when they had eaten enough, they lightened the ship and threw out the wheat into the sea."],[2015.85,"So there's no more food at all."],[2017.39,"So they're so at this point, they're so confident what St."],[2021.15,"Paul is telling them that they eat their fill, and then the rest of the food they just dump to further lighten the ship because they believe what he's telling them."],[2033.03,"When it was day, they did not recognize the land, but they observed a bay with a beach onto which they plan to run the ship if possible."],[2041.99,"Okay. So they see land, but they have no idea what land it is where they are."],[2048.28,"Um but I say, okay, we'll run, we'll we'll try and run the ship right up onto the beach."],[2055.66,"We're gonna have to beach it there."],[2057.18,"When they say try, that means hopefully we're not gonna run into anything else on the way to the beach."],[2061.6,"That's gonna wreck the ship before we get there."],[2066.52,"And they let go the anchors and left them in the sea."],[2070.68,"Meanwhile, loosing the rudder ropes, and they hoisted the mainsail to the wind and made for shore."],[2076.36,"Okay, so they just cut they they figure they're losing the ship one way or another, so they just dropped the anchors completely to further light the ship."],[2085.0,"They put the sail up to try and get some speed going and just head as fast as they can toward that beach."],[2092.92,"But striking a place where two seas met, they ran the ship aground and the prowl stuck fast and remained immovable."],[2100.36,"But the stern was being broken up by the violence of the waves."],[2105.96,"So they hit something."],[2108.78,"There's some kind of natural barrier there."],[2110.82,"It doesn't say exactly what it was."],[2112.18,"Was it a reef or whether it was rock or whatever?"],[2115.7,"That was uh sort of a border between two uh bodies of water, which would be like maybe there was a lagoon, you know, that was ringed in on the side of the island, and this uh these rocks sort of form a border between that lagoon and the sea, the sea open sea proper."],[2137.3,"So they run aground on that."],[2140.18,"The uh the front of the ship is wedged, the back of the ship is now getting hit by all the waves and everything, and is starting to break apart, and the soldiers' plan was to kill the prisoners lest any of them should swim away and escape."],[2154.74,"Now, this isn't just cruelty"],[2162.06,"It is cruelty because you know they're Roman."],[2164.62,"But if you had were in charge of a prisoner and the prisoner escaped, it was the death penalty for you as a soldier."],[2176.78,"So the soldiers are thinking, look, I'm not gonna risk it."],[2180.78,"You know, if it's my neck or theirs, I don't even have to stop and think."],[2186.52,"It's you, not me."],[2188.12,"Who's gonna who's gonna die here?"],[2191.32,"But the centurion, wanting to save Paul, kept them from their purpose and commanded that those who could swim should jump overboard first and get to land."],[2199.32,"So the centurion steps in to protect St"],[2206.16,"Paul, and as the commanding officer, if he says, uh-uh, that's uh-uh, you know."],[2214.56,"So he says, everybody who can swim, jump off the ship, head for land."],[2222.48,"And the rest, some on boards and some on parts of the ship, and so it was that they all escaped safely to land."],[2228.72,"Okay, so the rest of them kind of fall into the water, they can't swim, but since the ship's all breaking up, they grab boards and planks and pieces of ship."],[2236.56,"Um and uh sort of like the end of Titanic, except two people can be on one."],[2242.24,"I don't know why two people couldn't."],[2244.0,"But anyway. Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2265.4,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com. Yeah."]],"o":[{"t":21.52,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Paul's trials so far","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Acts 27</b>; first Acts study online for <b>flashback</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Getting near the <b>end of the book</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Series of Paul's trials <b>one after the other</b> since Jerusalem arrest</li><li class=\"d0\">Returned to Jerusalem for <b>Pentecost</b>; troublemakers from missionary journeys showed up</li><li class=\"d0\">At <b>St. James's suggestion</b> sponsored men under <b>Nazirite vows</b> at the temple</li><li class=\"d1\">public show: Paul <b>not rejecting</b> Jewish traditions, embracing them</li><li class=\"d0\">Rumor: he brought a <b>Greek into the temple</b> → mob, beating</li><li class=\"d0\">Rescued by <b>Roman soldiers</b> - meant to beat him too until learned <b>Roman citizen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ house arrest in the barracks</li></ul>"},{"t":139.52,"lv":3,"h":"Caesarea and the governors","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Centurion sent him to <b>Caesarea</b>, to the governor - out of immediate danger</li><li class=\"d0\">Governor <b>replaced</b>; new one kept Paul in prison</li><li class=\"d0\">Several trials; <b>Herod Agrippa</b> visits</li><li class=\"d1\">Roman governor knew it was <b>Jewish religion</b> but not much about it</li><li class=\"d1\">hoped Agrippa could <b>explain</b> what it was about</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's goal even before arrest: <b>go to Rome</b>, proclaim gospel to <b>Caesar</b></li><li class=\"d1\">used every trial to <b>proclaim the gospel</b> to his judges: Agrippa, two governors</li></ul>"},{"t":190.04,"lv":3,"h":"Appeal to Caesar and Agrippa's verdict","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul had <b>appealed to Caesar</b> - his right as citizen</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't mean you'd actually <b>see Caesar</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Governor must send <b>letter to Caesar</b> explaining charges - <b>couldn't come up with any</b></li><li class=\"d1\">seemed Jewish law and custom, not <b>Roman law</b>; Paul broke none</li><li class=\"d0\">Agrippa: Paul had <b>very nearly converted</b> him - \"in such a short time\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>\"I got nothing\"</b> in terms of charge</li><li class=\"d1\">\"could have been set free, except for the <b>appeal to Caesar</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Governor still responsible: appeal made and <b>not sent on</b> = violating protocol</li></ul>"},{"t":293.64,"lv":3,"h":"Years of protective custody","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Chapter 27 begins packing Paul off to Rome</li><li class=\"d0\">Trial process has gone on <b>a few years</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mainly <b>house arrest</b> / <b>protective custody</b> within Roman territory</li><li class=\"d1\">people still trying to <b>kill him</b> for reasons Romans don't understand</li><li class=\"d0\">He <b>receives visitors</b>, can write - <b>some epistles written in this period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">just can't come and go; <b>for his own safety</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Roman citizen <b>assassinated in prison</b> after appealing to Caesar → bad for the governor</li><li class=\"d0\">→ they have to <b>protect him</b></li></ul>"},{"t":373.52,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:1-2 - Julius the centurion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:1</b> decided to sail to <b>Italy</b>; Paul and other prisoners handed to <b>Julius</b>, centurion of the <b>Augustan regiment</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Augustan regiment</b> = most important unit with reps in the area</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Praetorian Guard</b> guards Caesar himself - top of Roman military</li><li class=\"d1\">in outlying province like <b>Judea</b>, Augustan officers are <b>the VIPs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Julius now <b>responsible</b> to keep Paul safe and <b>get him to Rome</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:2</b> ship of <b>Adramyttium</b>, sailing <b>along the coast of Asia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no sailing straight across the Mediterranean then</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Asia\" = <b>Asia Minor</b>, now <b>Turkey</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Plan: up the coast of Palestine, around, <b>around Greece</b>, over to Italy</li></ul>"},{"t":478.52,"lv":3,"h":"Aristarchus and Luke aboard","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Aristarchus</b>, Macedonian of <b>Thessalonica</b>, with them</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>\"us\"</b> = <b>St. Luke</b> is there again</li><li class=\"d0\">Why allowed? Same reason Paul could <b>receive visitors</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not a Roman military ship</b> - a <b>merchant ship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">centurion <b>bought passage</b> for Paul (maybe just commandeered)</li><li class=\"d1\">Luke and Aristarchus <b>paid their own way</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aristarchus not heard of before - obviously <b>a Christian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ethnically Macedonian, lives in Thessalonica → presumably <b>convert of Paul's mission there</b></li></ul>"},{"t":571.24,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:3-8 - Sidon, Myra, Crete","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:3</b> next day <b>Sidon</b> (now <b>Lebanon</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">Julius <b>treated Paul kindly</b> - let him visit friends, receive care</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul knows people in most of these churches from earlier journeys</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:4</b> sailed under shelter of <b>Cyprus</b> - <b>contrary winds</b></li><li class=\"d1\">loop around Cyprus, deviating from course</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:5</b> off <b>Cilicia and Pamphylia</b>, came to <b>Myra</b> in Lycia</li><li class=\"d1\">same city where <b>St. Nicholas</b> later bishop - ~<b>250 years</b> later</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:6</b> centurion finds <b>Alexandrian ship</b> bound for Italy, puts them on it</li><li class=\"d1\">normal way to travel: <b>no passenger liners</b>, merchant ships carry cargo</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>book passage</b> - pay, or <b>volunteer to work the ship</b>, barter</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:7-8</b> slow sailing many days, trouble off <b>Cnidus</b>, wind not permitting</li><li class=\"d1\">sailed under shelter of <b>Crete</b> off <b>Salmone</b> - island as <b>windbreak</b>; better no wind than headwind; at least can use <b>oars</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:8</b> reached <b>Fair Havens</b>, near <b>Lasea</b></li></ul>"},{"t":796.17,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:9-10 - Paul's warning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:9</b> much time spent; sailing <b>dangerous</b> because the <b>fast</b> was already over</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish fast</b> (Day of Atonement) = marks the <b>time of year</b>, like \"near end of Lent\"; not about whether they were fasting</li><li class=\"d1\">weather getting <b>worse</b>, not better</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:10</b> Paul: \"I perceive this voyage will end with <b>disaster and much loss</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not just cargo and ship but <b>our lives</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cargo bad, ship worse, <b>life way worse</b>; drowning or <b>freezing in the water</b></li></ul>"},{"t":849.77,"lv":3,"h":"The ancient fear of the sea","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We don't fear the sea like they did - like <b>fear of flying</b> but worse; <b>their boats</b> would explain why</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no straight crossing</b> of the Mediterranean - wouldn't have made it</li><li class=\"d0\">In the <b>ancient Near East</b> the sea = <b>chaos, death, destruction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">storms, people who <b>never come back</b>, <b>unknown creatures</b>, dark, mysterious</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <b><i>yam</i></b> = sea; also name of <b>most evil Canaanite god</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some of that in NT symbolism - e.g. <b>Jesus walking on the water</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: <b>Poseidon</b> - one of <b>three most powerful</b> Greek gods, a force to be reckoned with, not \"Aquaman\"</li></ul>"},{"t":983.64,"lv":3,"h":"The abyss and pillars of the earth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">NT word <b>abyss</b> = <b>depths of the sea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">where <b>hell</b> was: demons begged not to be cast into the <b>abyss</b>, then ran the pigs <b>into the sea</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Revelation</b>: angel takes key to the abyss, <b>demons fly out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient Near East / Hebrew picture: <b>flat earth</b> surrounded by water</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dome of sky</b> above; water also <b>underneath</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pillars of the earth</b> in the water support dry land</li><li class=\"d1\">water below = <b>underworld</b> = abyss</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jonah</b>: fish swam down to the <b>pillars of the earth</b> → <b>descent into the underworld</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so <b>sign of Jonah</b> is more than \"three days\": <b>descent into death</b>, then freed</li></ul>"},{"t":1196.09,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:11-12 - Centurion sides with helmsman","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:11</b> centurion <b>more persuaded by helmsman and owner</b> than by Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">helmsman: \"I got this\"; owner needs <b>cargo to Rome</b> for his money</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:12</b> harbor <b>not suitable to winter</b> in - \"in spite of being called <b>Fair</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's advice: <b>stay for the winter</b>, sail in spring with better weather</li><li class=\"d1\">majority: don't want <b>three months</b> here</li><li class=\"d0\">Try to reach <b>Phoenix</b>, harbor of Crete opening <b>SW and NW</b>, and winter there</li><li class=\"d1\">better place to stop if they must</li></ul>"},{"t":1282.06,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:13-17 - The storm hits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:13</b> <b>south wind blew softly</b> - thought they'd obtained their desire; sailed close by Crete</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:14</b> <b>tempestuous headwind</b> called <b>Euroclydon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">named like <b>El Nino</b> or <b>Santa Ana winds</b> - a <b>seasonal wind</b>; some translations \"northeaster\"</li><li class=\"d1\">right-direction wind, then <b>boom, nor'easter</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:15-16</b> ship caught, couldn't head into wind - <b>let her drive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ran under shelter of small island <b>Clauda</b> as windbreak; got anchored near shore</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>secured the skiff</b> with difficulty - wind blowing it off the ship</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:17</b> <b>cables to undergird the ship</b> - reinforce it from underneath</li><li class=\"d1\">feared running aground on <b>Syrtis sands</b> (sandbars)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>struck sail</b>, let the boat go where wind and current carry it</li></ul>"},{"t":1460.49,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:18-20 - Cargo overboard, hope given up","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:18</b> next day <b>lightened the ship</b> - cargo overboard</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:19</b> third day <b>ship's tackle</b> (rigging) thrown overboard <b>with our own hands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everyone helps - <b>Paul, Luke</b>, centurion, not just sailors</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:20</b> <b>no sun or stars for many days</b> - \"all hope that we would be saved was <b>finally given up</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't see sun or stars → <b>can't tell where they are</b> or which way to head</li><li class=\"d1\">random sailing would make it worse; <b>adrift</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1540.89,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:21-26 - Paul: \"I told you so\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:21</b> after <b>long abstinence from food</b> (they're <b>out of food</b>, not fasting) Paul stands among them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you should have <b>listened to me</b> and not sailed from Crete\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"a wonderful saint, but he is that guy who will tell you I told you so\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:22</b> but <b>take heart</b> - <b>no loss of life</b>, only the ship</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:23-24</b> <b>angel</b> stood by him: \"the God to whom I belong and whom I serve\"</li><li class=\"d1\">says <b>\"the God\"</b> because speaking to <b>Gentile pagans</b>: which one?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"You must be brought before Caesar\"</b>; God has granted him <b>all who sail with you</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:25</b> \"I <b>believe God</b> that it will be just as it was told me\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:26</b> must <b>run aground on a certain island</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1729.38,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:27-32 - Fourteenth night, sailors' escape","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:27</b> <b>14th night</b> - two weeks lost, driven up and down the <b>Adriatic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">about midnight sailors sense <b>land nearby</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:28</b> <b>soundings</b>: <b>20 fathoms</b>, then <b>15</b> → getting shallower = approaching land</li><li class=\"d1\">drop weight on a rope and measure depth</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:29</b> feared <b>rocks</b> → dropped <b>four anchors from the stern</b>, <b>prayed for day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't wreck on a reef or sandbar still a ways out</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:30</b> sailors try to <b>escape in the skiff</b> under pretense of putting out anchors from the prow</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:31</b> Paul to centurion: <b>\"unless these men stay in the ship, you cannot be saved\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:32</b> soldiers <b>cut the ropes</b> of the skiff, let it fall</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's promise depends on all staying - only hope left; else die of <b>starvation or dehydration</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1888.38,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:33-38 - Paul urges food; the Eucharist","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:33</b> dawn: Paul implores all to <b>eat</b> - <b>14th day</b> without food</li><li class=\"d1\">had been eating <b>minimally</b> to make it last; now coming to <b>end of no food</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:34</b> \"not a hair will fall from your head\" - <b>not literal</b>, means no harm will come</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:35</b> Paul <b>took bread, gave thanks, broke it, began to eat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">language same as Luke of Christ → <b>Paul celebrating the Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">probably only <b>two communicants</b> with him: <b>Aristarchus and Luke</b> - at least a couple</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"he doesn't give it to all the other people on the boat who aren't Christians\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:36-37</b> all encouraged, ate; <b>276 persons</b> aboard</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:38</b> ate enough, <b>threw the wheat into the sea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no food left at all → <b>confident</b> in what Paul told them</li></ul>"},{"t":2033.03,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 27:39-44 - Aground and ashore","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:39</b> day: <b>didn't recognize the land</b>; saw a <b>bay with a beach</b> - plan to run ship onto it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:40</b> cut anchors loose, left them in the sea; <b>loosed rudder ropes</b>, hoisted <b>mainsail</b></li><li class=\"d1\">losing the ship anyway → drop anchors, <b>speed for the beach</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:41</b> struck <b>place where two seas met</b> - bow stuck fast, <b>stern broken up by waves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reef or rock; maybe a <b>lagoon</b> ringed from the open sea</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:42</b> soldiers plan to <b>kill the prisoners</b> lest they swim away</li><li class=\"d1\">not just cruelty: prisoner escapes = <b>death penalty for the soldier</b>; \"my neck or theirs\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:43</b> <b>centurion, wanting to save Paul</b>, stops them - commanding officer</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>those who can swim jump first</b> and get to land</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>27:44</b> rest on <b>boards and pieces of the ship</b> → <b>all escaped safely to land</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"like the end of Titanic, except two people can be on one\"</li></ul>"}]});
